    def sell_to_bid(
        self,
        code: str,
        *,
        stock_type: str,
        quantity: int,
        reference: Optional[str] = None,
        portfolio_id: Optional[str] = None,
        min_price: Optional[float] = None,
        warehouse_code: Optional[str] = None,
        condition_status: Optional[str] = None,
        cost: Optional[float] = None,
        inbond_address_id: Optional[int] = None,
        extra_headers: Optional[Mapping[str, str]] = None,
        extra_query: Optional[Mapping[str, Any]] = None,
        extra_body: Optional[Mapping[str, Any]] = None,
        timeout: Union[float, httpx.Timeout, None] = None,
        max_retries: Optional[int] = None,
    ) -> Write[SellResult]:
        """Sell into a bid.

        Sell into one bid on Cru’s market now, at its price after commission, credited to your
        Cru account: a line of your portfolio, or new stock.

        **The bid you name, and offers:write as well.** There is no “sell at the best bid”: pick
        one from GET /market/wines/{lwin18}/bids by its requirements. The sale needs
        offers:write besides orders:write. To buy an offer instead, POST
        /market/offers/{code}/buy.

        **A partner key should send min_price.** A partner’s sale has no preview, so min_price
        is what holds the price you read from the book. If the bid pays less, nothing is sold.

        **Wine held outside Cru, not in your portfolio yet.** For accounts Cru has set up to
        sell it, as the website’s Sell Now does: send stock_type external and warehouse_code,
        with no portfolio_id, and Cru creates the units at that warehouse and fills the bid with
        them at once — or deletes them again, and sells nothing, if the fill fails. You set no
        price: you receive the bid’s price less that warehouse’s settlement fee — its
        settlement_fee in GET /warehouses, such as GBP 6.00 per unit at UK_DRAK — and a
        corporate account pays none. Selling confirms the wine is free from material faults, in
        its original packaging, in a professional warehouse and not duty paid, and that you will
        transfer it to that warehouse; Cru emails you the purchase order. A bid that takes
        landed stock only cannot be filled this way.

        **Your own stock, as a supplier.** A supplier whose offer feed Cru imports can sell
        stock that is not in its feed: send stock_type supplier, with no portfolio_id. Cru adds
        an offer of just that quantity to your feed, at the feed’s warehouse, verified and not
        duty paid, and fills the bid with it at once, so selling it all leaves nothing on sale.
        If it cannot fill the bid, or the fill fails, it is taken back and nothing is sold.
        Supplier stock, new or a line of your portfolio, fills only bids placed on Cru.

        Scope: orders:write.

        A partner credential's write runs at once and returns the result in .result; a connected
        app's is staged, with the code to confirm in .confirmation.

        Args:
            code: The bid’s opaque code, from GET /market/bids. URL-encode it as a single path
                segment.
            reference: Your idempotency key: letters and digits, then . _ : or - allowed.
                Required on a partner credential’s write and kept for good, so a retry with it
                can never act twice. Ignored for connected apps.
            stock_type: What you are selling: cru, wine stored with Cru; external, wine you hold
                outside Cru; or supplier, your own stock as a supplier whose offer feed Cru
                imports. Anything else, bpo included, is refused with VALIDATION_INVALID_VALUE:
                BPO stock cannot be sold through the API.
            portfolio_id: A line of your portfolio, from GET /portfolio or GET /offers, of this
                bid’s wine and of the stock_type you send: its units are sold, and only those.
                Required for cru, as wine stored with Cru is always sold from your portfolio.
                Wine held outside Cru fills a bid only while its line is listed, as on the Cru
                website. Leave it out to sell new external or supplier stock.
            quantity: Units to sell: with portfolio_id, at most what the line has that the bid
                can take.
            min_price: The lowest price per unit you will take, after Cru’s commission, in the
                market’s currency — price as GET /market/bids shows it, less the settlement fee
                for wine held outside Cru — to at most 2 decimal places.
            warehouse_code: New stock only, and needed for it: the Cru warehouse wine you hold
                outside Cru, not in your portfolio yet, is delivered to — one of the bid’s
                deliverable_warehouses that GET /warehouses marks sells_your_inbond_wine for
                you. Only with stock_type external.
            condition_status: New external or supplier stock only: verified, the one condition
                that can fill a bid, and the default.
            cost: New stock only: what you paid per unit, in the market’s currency. Only with
                stock_type external.
            inbond_address_id: New stock only: the inbond warehouse the wine is in now, from GET
                /warehouses/inbond. Leave it out if you are not sure where the wine is: Cru
                records it as not sure, as the website’s Sell Now does. Only with stock_type
                external.
            extra_headers: Headers to add to, or replace, the SDK's own, for this call only.
            extra_query: Query parameters this version does not know yet, sent as given.
            extra_body: Body fields this version does not know yet, sent as given.
            timeout: Seconds, or an httpx.Timeout, for this call only. None keeps the client's.
            max_retries: Retries after the first attempt, for this call only. None keeps the
                client's.
        """
        return self._client._call(
            _SELL_TO_MARKET_BID,
            {'code': code},
            body={
                'reference': reference,
                'stock_type': stock_type,
                'portfolio_id': portfolio_id,
                'quantity': quantity,
                'min_price': min_price,
                'warehouse_code': warehouse_code,
                'condition_status': condition_status,
                'cost': cost,
                'inbond_address_id': inbond_address_id,
            },
            extra_headers=extra_headers,
            extra_query=extra_query,
            extra_body=extra_body,
            timeout=timeout,
            max_retries=max_retries,
        )


